# Development Mode: Break Compatibility Is OK

This application is in active development and has no public/stable release to support.
So you do not need to keep backwards compatibility or maintain an earlier version.

- Prefer removing legacy/compatibility code over keeping shims.
- When you change shared contracts (API/WebSocket/types), update all in-repo consumers together.
- Do not add versioned endpoints/events (`v1/v2`, `old/new`) unless the user explicitly asks for it.
- Keep refactors moving fast: correctness and internal consistency are the priority.

## Examples

- ❌ Bad: implement `wsEventV1` just to support older clients.
- ✅ Good: update the single `wsEvent` contract on both server and client, and adjust callers/tests in-repo.
